# Line endings.
#
# The shell scripts must be checked out with LF on every platform. A .sh or
# .command file with CRLF endings fails on macOS and Linux with
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" — which reads as a broken script
# rather than as a line-ending problem, and is exactly how a one-click
# installer stops being one click.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf

# The reverse for Windows scripts: cmd.exe tolerates LF in most places but not
# in every one, and a .bat is only ever run on Windows anyway.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.iss       text eol=crlf

# Everything else keeps whatever the working tree has, so the existing mixed
# CRLF/LF sources are not rewritten wholesale.
* -text

# Binary, never diffed or converted.
*.png       binary
*.ico       binary
*.icns      binary
*.hdr       binary
*.exr       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.gpxt      binary
